    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d212">

                <pb facs="0268" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.00/2407: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-26T16:00:00-03:00">February 26,
                            1944—4 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:47 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>551. I saw Benavides<anchor n="67" xml:id="d212-f1"/> who is recommending to his
                    Government that they take no action that in any way could be interpreted as
                    recognition of the new regime pending a very careful examination of all facts
                    surrounding yesterday’s events. Benavides feels that crux of situation is
                    whether Ramírez made a voluntary transfer of power to Farrell. He is himself
                    convinced that it was not voluntary and therefore a <hi rendition="#italic">coup
                        d’état</hi>. He remarked that actually we had no verification that Ramírez
                    had signed any document, citing case of Peñaranda.<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d212-f2"/> He therefore thinks it most important that Dean of
                    American Diplomatic Corps, Uruguayan Ambassador,<anchor n="69" xml:id="d212-f3"
                    /> should personally call on Ramírez to ascertain true story of what happened.
                    Benavides told me that plain-clothes detective has been posted by police in
                    front of his Embassy and when questioned admitted he had been placed there to
                    prevent Ramírez from seeking asylum there. He thinks that Government suspects
                    Ramírez will attempt to seek asylum or tell his story to some member of
                    Diplomatic Corps which he feels is in itself evidence that Ramírez did not act
                    voluntarily.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Montevideo, Santiago and Lima.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d212-f1" n="67" xml:id="d212-f1-content">
                <p>Marshal Oscar Benavides, Peruvian Ambassador in Argentina.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d212-f2" n="68" xml:id="d212-f2-content">
                <p>Gen. Enrique Peñaranda y del Castillo, former President of Bolivia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d212-f3" n="69" xml:id="d212-f3-content">
                <p>Eugenio Martínez Thedy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
